{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nCanonical bridge operations use conventional statuses: 0 for success or benign no-work/yield/paused states, 1 for operational failure, and 2 for invalid invocation/configuration. Legacy reconcile routes historically emit 3 for another live pass, 4 for the phase gate, and 75 for reschedule. The merged implementation centralizes semantic classification while retaining every legacy translation required by older workflows and environments.\n\nCanonical rebar bridge preview/sync and legacy rebar reconcile or direct python -m rebar_reconciler routes consume one typed disposition and differ only at the outer numeric adapter. Python and MCP bridge facades use the same core result; legacy Python rebar.reconcile and MCP reconcile continuity remains.\n\n## Exact contract\n\nCanonical preview/sync map converged, paused, in-flight, reschedule, and historical phase-gate states to 0; invalid invocation/configuration to 2; and operational failure to 1. Their state markers remain stable.\n\nLegacy CLI and direct-engine adapters map the same dispositions to the established 0/3/4/75/1/2 results with legacy messages and defaults. Older checked-out workflows, systemd/Jenkins units, scripts, and probes therefore continue to work. Retirement of the legacy codes is a separate future deprecation.\n\nThe production workflow uses the canonical route and no numeric whitelist. Preview remains non-mutating; sync preserves its existing mutation semantics; paused state still skips commit-and-push.\n\n## Verification\n\nRepository proof at merged ref 694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d:\n- src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py defines one disposition and separate canonical and legacy exits.\n- docs/exit-codes.md lines 29-54 and docs/release-notes.md lines 29-46 publish canonical 0/1/2, retained 3/4/75, and rolling-deployment continuity.\n- test_reconcile_main.py, test_bridge_cli_heldout.py, test_lock_steal.py, and test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py cover canonical/legacy subprocess and workflow behavior.\n- Public library and MCP contract suites cover both new bridge and retained reconcile facades.\n- The numeric-consumer census found no unclassified production 3/4/75 consumer; intentional uses remain only in legacy adapters, compatibility tests, and dual-contract documentation.\n\nNo new contention harness is required because the change classifies a result after existing lock/CAS seams and introduces no retry, cache, or composed write.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Canonical preview/sync emit only 0/1/2; benign dispositions exit 0 and no canonical adapter emits 3/4/75.\n- [x] Legacy reconcile CLI and direct-engine mode routes retain historical 3/4/75 translations, messages, and defaults.\n- [x] Canonical and legacy adapters consume one typed disposition, run the pass once, and preserve repository/ref effects.\n- [x] Python and MCP bridge and legacy consumers use the shared result boundary without a divergent whitelist.\n- [x] The production workflow uses canonical 0/1/2, has no old case whitelist, and preserves paused commit-skip behavior.\n- [x] Deployment, workflow, script, and probe consumers of 3/4/75 are migrated or explicitly classified as compatibility.\n- [x] Exit-code and release documentation publish both contracts and the rolling-migration guarantee.\n- [x] [operator-attested] Gerrit change 1595 and GitHub Actions run 31300543302 passed LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, and the focused subprocess, workflow, library, and MCP continuity suites at merged ref 694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786439835146221001,"uuid":"4cd9ca6a-3383-4fa8-8165-a399c3750884"}